# Commands run by the launchd job com.redwood.demo (RC_JOBS=rc.jobs.argbuf).
#
# WHAT THIS BOOT IS FOR: MTLArgumentEncoder, and the buffer OFFSET that goes with it.
#
# The frontend's half of argument buffers landed first and took Resident Evil 4 from 258
# translated shader libraries to 2 990 (docs/air.md 8.2.1). Those shaders still could not
# be USED, because an application binds their resources through an MTLArgumentEncoder and
# this driver answered none of its sixteen selectors. It answers them now, as a RECORDER
# rather than a writer -- the frontend resolved every member of every set to a binding
# while translating, and Metal's 8-byte handles are an Apple GPU's descriptors, which a
# Gallium driver cannot read -- and docs/air.md 8.2.2 is the whole of why.
#
# Everything here has run on this machine already, on softpipe, through the same contract
# (tools/air/argbufruncheck.sh: 20 checks, every one passing). What the phone adds is what
# softpipe cannot say: whether Metal's own classes hand the driver what it needs, and
# whether the Adreno fetches a uniform block from an offset.
#
# WHAT IT NEEDS
#
#   gpu=1              in XL_CMDLINE, or there is no Adreno and no compiler library
#                      answer; the driver then says so by name and every line below is
#                      NOT MEASURED rather than a failure.
#   GL=1 REST=1        in the image, so that /redwood/lib/libredwoodair.dylib is in it,
#                      and it must be a library built from THIS commit: the encoder's
#                      records live in that library, and an older one has no
#                      rwa_argbuf_encoded_members (metal_drv_test says so by name and
#                      stops, which is the honest reading and not a failure of the GPU).
#
# THE THREE MEASUREMENTS, each worth a different thing, and each printed apart.
#
# (a) THE FUNCTION PATH. -[MTLFunction newArgumentEncoderWithBufferIndex:] is a method of
#     METAL'S OWN class, and Metal's own implementation cannot work on this port: it asks
#     the function for its reflection, which asks the device for -compiler, which is a
#     two-instruction stub answering nil (-[_MTLDevice compiler], 0x181aac128), and then
#     reports "bufferIndex %lu does not identify an argument buffer" and returns nil
#     (0x181aef8a4, its failure path at 0x181aef924). So the driver answers it instead, by
#     REPLACING that method at run time (class_replaceMethod on
#     objc_lookUpClass("_MTLFunction"), from ensure_spi) and reading the function's own
#     bitcode -- which is the one thing this port has and Metal's reflection has not. Not
#     a category: a category would make the dylib import _OBJC_CLASS_$__MTLFunction, and a
#     private class symbol that does not bind is a dylib that does not LOAD, which would
#     take the whole driver down for every Metal client rather than just this selector.
#
#     Apple's shaders declare NO argument buffer (0 of the 456 modules of iOS 16.5's
#     corpus), so nil is still the right answer here. What the boot measures is WHOSE nil
#     it is: the driver's sentence says how many sets the function has, and Metal's does
#     not. If the line with "this function declares 0" is missing, the replacement did not
#     take effect and nothing else about the function path is believable.
#
# (b) THE DESCRIPTOR PATH, which needs no shader at all:
#     -[MTLDevice newArgumentEncoderWithArguments:] with a pointer, a texture and a
#     sampler member. encodedLength must be 24 and alignment 8 -- eight bytes per member,
#     which is what Apple's own metadata says a set is: over Resident Evil 4's 7 858 sets,
#     7 856 declare `air.arg_type_size` equal to 8 x members and all 7 858 declare
#     `air.arg_type_align_size` 8 (tools/air/argbuflen.py). The two that do not are both
#     in the one kernel that builds indirect command buffers, which this frontend refuses
#     before an encoder could be made for it.
#
#     Then the three members are encoded and the compiler library is asked how many
#     records it holds for that (buffer, offset): three. That count is the only thing an
#     encoder leaves behind here, because there is nothing written into the buffer to read
#     back.
#
# (c) THE BUFFER OFFSET, which is not about argument buffers and is why this boot is worth
#     spending: Apple's own clearFragment20, drawn twice, with its 48-byte constant block
#     at offset 0 of one buffer and at OFFSET 1164 of another whose other bytes are 0xa5.
#     1164 is the offset the window server binds its own fragment buffer 2 at -- 55 times
#     in one boot of 21 October -- and until this commit every one of those bindings was
#     refused ("a non-zero offset is not implemented"). The two draws must print the same
#     four words, and they must be the host's own reference:
#
#         2458616957 2930221209 3401825461 3873429713
#
#     1164 is a multiple of 4 (Metal's own rule) and of neither 16 nor 64, so this is the
#     path where the compiler library hands the block to the driver as a `user_buffer` and
#     freedreno uploads it itself (u_upload_data at an alignment of 64). An aligned offset
#     is passed straight through as the binding's own offset and nothing is copied; the
#     host run measures both (offset 64 and offset 4) and the phone measures the one that
#     matters to the window server.
#
# (the full path on every line: nothing here promises these go through a shell)
/redwood/bin/metal_drv_test --argbuf /System/Library/Extensions/AppleMetalGLRenderer.bundle/default.metallib
# The same three again, in a SECOND process of the same boot. Not a repeat for its own
# sake: the refusals this work replaces were the window server's, every line of this
# driver now carries the process that wrote it, and two pids in one log is the cheapest
# way to see that the prefix is per process and not per library. The library is the same
# one -- clearFragment20 is in it, and AV1SW.metallib has no fragment shader at all, so
# asking that one would measure nothing and say so.
/redwood/bin/metal_drv_test --argbuf /System/Library/Extensions/AppleMetalGLRenderer.bundle/default.metallib
#
# HOW TO READ THE LOG (logs have NUL bytes, so grep -a always):
#
#   grep -a 'drvtest: argbuf:' logs/*-run.log
#       every line of the three measurements. The four that must say ok, as whole
#       substrings to grep for:
#         'ok: encodedLength 24'
#         'ok: 3 of 3 members recorded against that buffer at offset 0'
#         'ok: the uniform block at offset 0 draws'
#         'ok: the uniform block at offset 1164, the window server's own draws'
#       and each draw line ends in the four words it got, so a WRONG one names the block it
#       read instead of merely counting a failure.
#
#   grep -a 'this function declares' logs/*-run.log
#       (a): the driver's own answer to newArgumentEncoderWithBufferIndex:. Absent means
#       Metal's own implementation answered instead, so the replacement did not take
#       effect -- and then the dylib in that image is older than this list.
#
#   grep -a 'redwoodmetal\[' logs/*-run.log
#       every sentence of the driver, each now carrying the process that provoked it.
#       This is the boot to check that on: four or five Metal clients are alive.
#
#   grep -a 'a non-zero offset is not implemented' logs/*-run.log
#       MUST BE EMPTY. That sentence is deleted by this commit; a hit means the image
#       carries an older libredwoodmetal.dylib than this list.
#
#   grep -a 'was never filled through an MTLArgumentEncoder' logs/*-run.log
#       the refusal this feature is mostly made of. Expected empty in this boot -- nothing
#       here draws with a set -- and if the window server ever binds an argument buffer it
#       will appear here, which is exactly what it is for.
#
#   grep -a 'does not identify an argument buffer' logs/*-run.log
#       both (a)'s answer and Metal's own wording. Read the whole line: ours continues
#       "this function declares 0".
#
#   grep -a 'uploaded\|user_buffer\|multiples of' logs/*-run.log
#       any refusal about an offset. "in multiples of" would be a `device` buffer at an
#       offset the GPU cannot fetch from, which nothing here binds; a hit names the shader.
#
# WHAT THIS BOOT DOES NOT ANSWER, said here so nobody reads it as more:
#
#   - NO DRAW GOES THROUGH AN ARGUMENT BUFFER ON THE DEVICE. That needs a .metallib holding
#     a shader that declares one, and this port has no container writer: Apple's libraries
#     have none (0 of 456) and the game's are not something a job list can reach. The
#     draw-through-the-encoder differential is the host's, on softpipe
#     (tools/air/argbufruncheck.sh), where the same picture comes out of an encoder as out
#     of direct arguments and every deliberately wrong record prints its own named
#     picture. What the device adds is (a), (b) and (c).
#   - NOTHING IS TIMED. What a recorded binding costs against a direct one is unmeasured;
#     the records are a linked list walked once per set per draw, and an application with
#     thousands of encodings in one buffer is the thing to measure before it is a table.
#   - THE SAMPLER AND TEXTURE MEMBER INDICES are checked on the host, by drawing one
#     texture through two samplers and two textures through one shader; the device cannot
#     check them until (b) above has a shader behind it.
